Refusal of licence. - The Central Government or the Chief Controller of
Imports and Exports may refuse to grant a licence or direct any other
licensing authority not to grant a licence :-
(a) if the application for the licence does not confers to any provision of
this Order;
(b) if such application contains any false or fraudulent or misleading
statement;
(c) if the applicant uses in support of the application any document which
is false or fabricated or which has been tempered with;
(d) if the applicant on any occasion has tempered with an export licence or
has exported goods without a licence where it is necessary, or has been a
party to any corrupt or fraudulent practice in his commercial dealings;
(e) if the application for an export licence is defective and does not
conform to the prescribed rules;
(f) if the applicant commits a breach of the Export Trade Control
Regulations;
(g) if the appellant is not eligible for a licence in accordance with the
Export Trade Control Regulations;
(h) if the licensing authority decides to canalise exports through special
or specialized agencies or channels;
